      content: '**True Relapse vs. Pseudo-Relapse**:\n\nA **true relapse (exacerbation)** is defined as:\n- New or worsening neurologic symptoms lasting **>24 hours**\n- Occurring **>30 days** from the onset of the last relapse\n- **Not attributable** to infection, fever, or metabolic derangement\n- Typically develops over days and peaks within 2-4 weeks\n\nA **pseudo-relapse** involves:\n- Temporary worsening of existing or prior symptoms\n- Triggered by **infection** (especially UTI — most common in MS), fever, heat exposure (Uhthoff phenomenon), stress, sleep deprivation, or medication changes\n- Resolves when the triggering factor is treated\n- Does NOT represent new CNS inflammatory activity\n\n**In this patient**: The progressive onset over 5 days involving a new deficit (right leg weakness not previously affected) is concerning for a true relapse, but pseudo-relapse must be excluded first.\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n1. **Urinalysis and urine culture** — UTI is the most common trigger for pseudo-relapse in MS\n2. **CBC with differential** — screen for infection or other hematologic abnormality\n3. **Comprehensive metabolic panel** — electrolytes, renal and hepatic function\n4. **MRI brain and cervical/thoracic spine with and without gadolinium** — look for new or enlarging T2/FLAIR lesions and gadolinium-enhancing lesions indicating active inflammation. A new enhancing lesion confirms a true relapse.\n5. **Vitamin D level (25-OH)** — low levels are associated with increased relapse risk\n6. **TSH** — hypothyroidism can mimic MS fatigue\n7. **JC virus antibody titer** — important for DMT risk stratification if therapy change is considered\n\n**MRI Findings Expected in True Relapse**:\n- New T2 hyperintense lesion(s) in the thoracic or cervical spinal cord (correlating with right leg weakness)\n- Gadolinium enhancement indicating active blood-brain barrier breakdown (enhancing lesions typically persist for 2-8 weeks)\n- Comparison with prior MRI to confirm new lesion burden',
