      content: '**Acute Relapse Treatment**:\n1. **IV methylprednisolone 1000 mg daily for 3-5 days** — standard first-line treatment for acute MS relapse. Accelerates recovery but does not change the ultimate degree of recovery. Administer in the morning to minimize insomnia.\n2. **Oral taper** — consider a short oral prednisone taper (e.g., 60 mg x 5 days then taper over 1-2 weeks) though this is optional and practice varies.\n3. **Gastroprotection**: Proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole 20 mg daily) during steroid course.\n4. **Blood glucose monitoring**: Especially important as steroids cause hyperglycemia. Check fasting glucose daily during infusion.\n5. **If refractory to steroids**: Consider plasmapheresis (PLEX) — 5-7 exchanges over 10-14 days. Indicated for severe relapses that do not respond to IV steroids.\n\n**Disease-Modifying Therapy Review**:\n- Patient has had a **breakthrough relapse on dimethyl fumarate** — this warrants discussion of escalating to a **higher-efficacy DMT** (e.g., natalizumab, ocrelizumab, or ofatumumab).\n- Check **JC virus antibody index** before considering natalizumab (risk of PML if JCV positive with index >1.5).\n- Discuss **treatment goals**: Achieving NEDA (No Evidence of Disease Activity) — no relapses, no new MRI lesions, no disability progression.\n\n**Fatigue Management**:\n1. **Pharmacologic**: Amantadine 100 mg BID (first-line for MS fatigue). Alternative: modafinil 100-200 mg each morning.\n2. **Non-pharmacologic**: Energy conservation techniques, pacing strategies, cooling vests for heat sensitivity, sleep hygiene counseling.\n3. **Exercise**: Aerobic exercise 30 minutes, 3-5 days per week at moderate intensity (60-75% max HR). Swimming or aquatic therapy excellent for heat-sensitive patients. Resistance training 2-3 days per week.\n\n**Spasticity Management**:\n- Stretching program for right lower extremity — daily hamstring, calf, and hip flexor stretches\n- Consider baclofen 5 mg TID, titrate as needed (max 80 mg/day). Monitor for weakness and sedation.\n- Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) evaluation for right foot drop to improve gait safety\n\n**Rehabilitation Referrals**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Gait training, balance exercises, strengthening, aerobic conditioning\n- **Occupational therapy**: Energy conservation education, workplace modifications\n- **Neuropsychology**: Formal cognitive assessment given low SDMT score\n- **Psychology/psychiatry**: Mood support given recent divorce and disease burden',
